{
  "id": "Compound::DB00544 - Disease::DOID:1612",
  "verbalization": "Fluorouracil, a nucleic acid synthesis inhibitor, is linked to Breast Cancer through its inclusion in the class of compounds that includes Capecitabine, which itself is known to treat Breast Cancer. This relationship is supported by the shared lowest common ancestor of Fluorouracil and Capecitabine, which is the category of Organic Cyclic Compounds and Pyrimidine Antagonists. The evidence suggests that Fluorouracil and its derivative, Capecitabine, exert their therapeutic effects through mechanisms that target nucleic acid synthesis, thereby inhibiting the growth of cancer cells. \n\nAdditionally, Fluorouracil's ability to treat Head And Neck Cancer, which is then treated by Vinblastine, which in turn affects Breast Cancer, further underscores a broader pathway of action. The shared organic cyclic compound and pyrimidine antagonist properties provide a structural basis for the drug's mechanism of action across different cancers. While the exact molecular pathways may differ, the commonality of these chemical properties and their role in cancer treatment suggests a potential for repurposing Fluorouracil in the context of Breast Cancer.",
